      "side": 1,
      "message": "The code here clamps the lowest battery SOC to BATTERY_LEVEL_NEAR_FULL \u003d\u003d 97 when AC is plugged.\nNote that the battery level in UI is 3% higher than the real measurement from EC.\nAnd the lowest battery SOC we\u0027ve seen when AC is plugged so far is 96% (as described in the bug link).\nSo if we unplugged AC with battery SOC clamped to 97% (while UI still shows 100%), we expect to see battery SOC in UI drops abruptly from 100% to 99% (96% + 3%).\nThis is not terrible.\n\nHowever, you brought up a good point.\nThere are edge cases where we can\u0027t catch charge_termination event is cleared.\ne.g. charging stops due to high temp, discharging on AC (charge control host command)...\nSo we will probably clamp SOC when we shouldn\u0027t, and battery SOC can drop significantly below BATTERY_LEVEL_NEAR_FULL.\n\nThis goes back to the fact that we implement rt946x_is_charge_done() based on interrupts.\nBut rt946x only supports interrupt for the start of charge termination (CHG_TERMI), but not\nfor the end of charge termination.\nThere is an interrupt for CHG_RECHGI (Re-Charge behavior), but it\u0027s not exhaustive enough to cover all the cases of \u0027the end of charge termination\u0027.\n\nTherefore we should implement rt946x_is_charge_done() based on polling in CL:1044768.",
